00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:28.590
So Apps Script is a
serverless JavaScript runtime

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:32.520
for G suite automation,
extension, and integration.

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:35.250
The first key part
here is serverless.

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:35.920
And serverless.

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:38.970
And serverless has
as many definitions

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:41.130
as it has people
who use the word.

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:44.640
But the key point is not that
it doesn't run on servers.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:46.050
Obviously, it runs on servers.

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:49.500
It's that you have no role
in provisioning or managing

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:51.120
those servers.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:52.609
I think another
key piece of this--

00:04:52.609 --> 00:04:54.150
and this got talked
about yesterday--

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:58.050
is that server lease
applications are event driven.

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:01.620
You don't have to proactively
call this application

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:03.180
from other code
because then you're

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:04.440
still managing a
server somewhere

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:05.481
that's running some code.

00:05:05.481 --> 00:05:08.610
And so many of the use
cases for Apps Script

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:10.680
are those where the
code that you write

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:14.610
is triggered at the right
moment by a user behavior,

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:17.310
by a change to a document,
without your having to write

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:20.530
the code that triggers it.

00:05:20.530 --> 00:05:21.150
JavaScript.

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:25.140
This is a key piece today, and
at least for the near term.

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:30.560
Apps Script is
written in JavaScript.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:33.980
And then the last part I want to
highlight is G Suite affinity.

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:35.480
That's not to say
that you can't use

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:36.950
Apps Script to do other things.

00:05:36.950 --> 00:05:40.760
You can hit any RESTful
API with an URL fetch call.

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:42.920
You can talk to JDBC.

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:46.520
You can talk to a bunch of
Google services via our built

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:50.900
in APIs, or directly to their
REST API via advanced services

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:52.400
and Apps Script.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:55.540
But our bread and
butter is G Suite.

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:58.310
And that means built in
APIs for talking to G Suite

00:05:58.310 --> 00:06:01.520
applications, for scripting
Gmail, Docs, Sheets,

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:07.100
Calendar, but also
event-driven behavior

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:10.610
based on those applications,
and extensions and add-ons

00:06:10.610 --> 00:06:12.870
to those applications.

00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:14.960
So G Suite is really
where Apps Script starts.

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:20.780
A few use cases.

00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:24.510
You know, JavaScript is a Turing
complete programming language.

00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:27.210
You can do just about
whatever you want to.

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:32.520
But in the enterprise, this is
really what we see happening.

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:36.570
One way that I might summarize
some of these, at least,

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:39.400
is that these are
applications that used to,

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:44.760
or often still do get done in
Excel or in Google Sheets where

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:46.710
you have a spreadsheet
that maybe has

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:49.480
some macros or some
scripts attached to it

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:52.110
that is doing more than a
spreadsheet ought to do.

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:54.630
And frankly, that's
often the way

00:06:54.630 --> 00:06:57.660
that an Apps Script
application itself evolves.

00:06:57.660 --> 00:06:59.700
You open the script
editor in Sheets.

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:01.470
You start typing some code.

00:07:01.470 --> 00:07:03.900
And before you know
it, your spreadsheet

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:06.450
is a mission
critical application

00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:08.850
for your team or
the whole company,

00:07:08.850 --> 00:07:12.240
and you have built your first
Apps Script application.

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:14.740
We'll talk a little bit about
where you might go from there,

00:07:14.740 --> 00:07:18.870
add a UI on top of it and make
it a full fledged application.

00:07:18.870 --> 00:07:21.150
And I'll also touch
briefly on App Maker, which

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:23.880
is a tool that was
presented yesterday

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:27.390
that's great for building UI
on top of Apps Script business

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:29.110
logic.

00:07:29.110 --> 00:07:32.820
But key use case is enterprise
workflows, especially

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:37.032
approval type workflows on
anything document-related.

00:07:37.032 --> 00:07:39.240
If you're on G Suite, you'll
find yourself automating

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:41.790
an Apps Script often.

00:07:41.790 --> 00:07:43.560
Resort management.

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:46.830
We internally use a tool
that's built in Apps Script

00:07:46.830 --> 00:07:51.300
to do product management on the
docs and drive team at Google.

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:53.700
And automation.

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:56.760
This is anything from writing
a little one off script

00:07:56.760 --> 00:08:02.220
to setting up a time trigger
to having a spreadsheet that

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:05.670
generates a report
on a bi-weekly basis.

00:08:05.670 --> 00:08:07.980
And reporting is right
there on the list too.

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:10.140
And then with 3P
integrations too,

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:15.000
if you want Sheets or Docs to
talk to a third party service,

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:18.480
then Apps Script is a great
way to accomplish that.

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:19.890
And then, lastly, domain admin.

00:08:19.890 --> 00:08:23.430
We have some APIs that let
you talk to the Admin API.

00:08:23.430 --> 00:08:26.270
If you were in the App
Maker session yesterday,

00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:30.150
they mentioned a
cool new team's tool

00:08:30.150 --> 00:08:33.510
that's being deployed as an
App Maker template that's

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:36.059
built on top of the
Admin SDK, which

00:08:36.059 --> 00:08:39.970
is accessible in Apps Script.

00:08:39.970 --> 00:08:41.010
OK.

00:08:41.010 --> 00:08:45.990
So there are four
different ways that folks

00:08:45.990 --> 00:08:48.480
come to use App
Script whether or not

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:50.950
you're aware that
you're using App Script.

00:08:50.950 --> 00:08:54.690
And by far the
biggest touchpoint

00:08:54.690 --> 00:08:59.040
is the Doc, Sheets and
Forms script editor.

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:01.560
So this is the script
editor that you

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:05.455
access by going to the Tools
Script Editor menu in Docs

00:09:05.455 --> 00:09:09.240
and Sheets, or the vertical
ellipsis Script Editor

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:11.820
menu in Forms.

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:15.435
That drops you into the script
editor in the specific context

00:09:15.435 --> 00:09:16.480
of that document.

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:20.610
So that lets you write some code
that operates on that document.

00:09:20.610 --> 00:09:22.530
You'll use the
script editor when

00:09:22.530 --> 00:09:25.860
you have a script that
has a special relationship

00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:27.660
to a document.

00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:31.720
And so this is that use case
where the sheet is basically

00:09:31.720 --> 00:09:35.220
the front end to
the application,

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:40.470
or where you are adding some
functionality to a document

00:09:40.470 --> 00:09:44.340
to build your table of
contents, for example.

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:47.580
And the way in which
that special relationship

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:50.760
is captured is with
these three methods.

00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:54.240
And so, depending on which
application you're scripting,

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:57.875
you'll call these methods to
get an object representing

00:09:57.875 --> 00:09:59.250
the current document
that you can

00:09:59.250 --> 00:10:00.630
use to act on that document.

00:10:04.330 --> 00:10:06.360
You also have access
to simple triggers,

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:08.190
which are super easy to code.

00:10:08.190 --> 00:10:10.980
You just define an on open
or an on added function.

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:16.350
That code runs every time that
event happens to your document.

00:10:16.350 --> 00:10:19.680
And so on edit in
particular, is good for doing

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:23.940
complex work that depends on
content across say, a sheet.

00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:26.760
You might use custom functions
for something piecemeal.

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:29.240
And so you can also
define a custom function

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:31.110
that you can call in
your sheet, and it

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:33.420
will operate on whichever
cells you reference

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:34.740
where you call it in the sheet.

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:37.115
If you want to do something
that is more broad based that

00:10:37.115 --> 00:10:38.910
sweeps in the whole
contents of the sheet,

00:10:38.910 --> 00:10:40.493
you might look to
the on edit trigger.

00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:48.470
The entry point number two is
directly at script.google.com

00:10:48.470 --> 00:10:52.010
You'll notice if you go to
Tool Script Editor you're

00:10:52.010 --> 00:10:55.010
editing a script at
script.google.com exactly

00:10:55.010 --> 00:10:57.920
the same product, just
a different context.

00:10:57.920 --> 00:11:02.540
And by and large, you can do
all of the exact same things

00:11:02.540 --> 00:11:04.280
in one of the standalone
scripts that you

00:11:04.280 --> 00:11:07.920
can do in a bound script.

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:11.330
Again, the decision tree
is, is this script--

00:11:11.330 --> 00:11:13.070
does it have a
special relationship

00:11:13.070 --> 00:11:14.760
with a particular document?

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:16.820
If so, Tool Script Editor.

00:11:16.820 --> 00:11:19.280
If not, or even if in
the future it might not

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:21.980
have a special relationship
to that one document,

00:11:21.980 --> 00:11:24.410
consider going straight
to script.google.com

00:11:24.410 --> 00:11:27.050
and using other means to
get access to the documents

00:11:27.050 --> 00:11:28.340
that you need to talk to.

00:11:28.340 --> 00:11:32.060
You can use the drive picker
to get a drive file ID.

00:11:32.060 --> 00:11:35.690
And you can use the
document app API

00:11:35.690 --> 00:11:37.940
to get a reference
to whatever document

00:11:37.940 --> 00:11:40.190
you want to talk to
in Docs, for example.

00:11:40.190 --> 00:11:42.080
And so that's kind
of the first decision

00:11:42.080 --> 00:11:43.550
that you'll make when
you're approaching a script

00:11:43.550 --> 00:11:45.260
is, is this script
always going to be

00:11:45.260 --> 00:11:47.631
attached to this one document.

00:11:47.631 --> 00:11:49.130
Here are some of
the things that you

00:11:49.130 --> 00:11:53.350
would do in a standalone
Apps Script project.

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:55.100
You can just write a
quick utility script.

00:11:55.100 --> 00:12:00.020
And so if you need to blast
50 emails or 100 emails

00:12:00.020 --> 00:12:02.630
to people in your
org based on rows

00:12:02.630 --> 00:12:07.070
in a spreadsheet, or some
external data source, say,

00:12:07.070 --> 00:12:08.330
write it up in Apps Script.

00:12:08.330 --> 00:12:09.680
Hit the Run button.

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:11.070
Test it out first.

00:12:11.070 --> 00:12:13.529
And send those emails off.

00:12:13.529 --> 00:12:15.320
You can also build web
apps in Apps Script.

00:12:15.320 --> 00:12:18.230
And this is something that
we see leveraged particularly

00:12:18.230 --> 00:12:19.550
heavily at Google.

00:12:19.550 --> 00:12:22.190
A ton of the tools
that we use internally

00:12:22.190 --> 00:12:24.350
are written in Apps Script.

00:12:24.350 --> 00:12:26.390
When the user hits
your web app, which

00:12:26.390 --> 00:12:28.910
is published at
a well-known URL,

00:12:28.910 --> 00:12:30.620
it runs your Apps Script code.

00:12:30.620 --> 00:12:32.450
And your Apps
Script code returns

00:12:32.450 --> 00:12:35.330
HTML back to the client.

00:12:35.330 --> 00:12:38.300
We have a templating
system that lets

00:12:38.300 --> 00:12:42.050
you easily code HDML
templates and populate them.

00:12:42.050 --> 00:12:44.240
And we'll actually
do a deep dive

00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:46.700
on this later in the
session, how you build a web

00:12:46.700 --> 00:12:48.410
app using Apps Script.

00:12:48.410 --> 00:12:52.160
And you can also build
writes to be web services.

00:12:52.160 --> 00:12:54.830
It's not something that
will go deep into today,

00:12:54.830 --> 00:12:57.230
but our execution
API lets you invoke

00:12:57.230 --> 00:13:01.530
Apps Script via a REST API
so you can build the back end

00:13:01.530 --> 00:13:03.960
service.

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:05.030
Lastly, add-ons.

00:13:05.030 --> 00:13:07.040
As mentioned, that
is going to get

00:13:07.040 --> 00:13:12.950
covered in CB304, which is
the next session in this room.

00:13:12.950 --> 00:13:14.930
Stick around if you
like what you hear,

00:13:14.930 --> 00:13:19.890
or maybe even if you don't,
because he's cooler than I am.

00:13:19.890 --> 00:13:21.060
OK.

00:13:21.060 --> 00:13:23.640
One place you may have
seen Apps Script before

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:26.580
without knowing that it was
Apps Script his AdWords scripts.

00:13:26.580 --> 00:13:29.010
AdWords scripts run in
the Apps Script runtime,

00:13:29.010 --> 00:13:31.670
and can leverage a bunch
of the Apps Script APIs.

00:13:34.810 --> 00:13:37.450
And then, lastly, AppMaker,
which I've mentioned,

00:13:37.450 --> 00:13:43.300
and which is being demoed across
the street in Moscone West.

00:13:43.300 --> 00:13:47.980
Incredible tool for
building web applications.

00:13:47.980 --> 00:13:50.430
Apps Script sits
behind AppMaker.

00:13:50.430 --> 00:13:54.780
Deployed AppMaker application is
an Apps Script web application.

00:13:54.780 --> 00:13:58.870
And as such, it has access
to all of the same APIs

00:13:58.870 --> 00:14:00.940
and facilities that
Apps Script does.

00:14:00.940 --> 00:14:04.150
When you write a server
side script in AppMaker,

00:14:04.150 --> 00:14:08.945
that is Apps Script code
that you're writing.

00:14:08.945 --> 00:14:11.450
If you want to learn more,
I think the website is

00:14:11.450 --> 00:14:16.060
GSuite.google.com/appmaker,
all one word.

00:14:16.060 --> 00:14:17.920
I definitely suggest
checking it out.

00:14:17.920 --> 00:14:19.795
This is the team we work
really closely with.

00:14:22.140 --> 00:14:23.040
OK.

00:14:23.040 --> 00:14:28.410
So we're going to do
some hands on coding.

00:14:28.410 --> 00:14:31.590
I hope it's not too slow.

00:14:31.590 --> 00:14:35.160
Stop me if you're
bored to tears.

00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:36.960
We're going to
organize a speaker

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:38.760
event, a little bit contrived.

00:14:38.760 --> 00:14:40.320
But it shows off a
bunch of the stuff

00:14:40.320 --> 00:14:43.086
you can do at Apps Script.

00:14:43.086 --> 00:14:44.460
By the way, we're
not going to be

00:14:44.460 --> 00:14:48.780
following along our
quick starts directly.

00:14:48.780 --> 00:14:52.290
But we are going to touch on
these three types of scripts

00:14:52.290 --> 00:14:54.240
that you see featured
in our documentation.

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:56.550
And also up there
is the web address.

00:14:56.550 --> 00:15:00.396
Apps Script documentation
is the best place to start,

00:15:00.396 --> 00:15:01.770
and certainly the
place you'll be

00:15:01.770 --> 00:15:07.500
coming back to as you go
deeper in Apps Script.

00:15:07.500 --> 00:15:08.090
OK.

00:15:08.090 --> 00:15:12.260
So we're going to proceed
through three versions

00:15:12.260 --> 00:15:16.880
of the same application
that introduce new types

00:15:16.880 --> 00:15:18.390
and levels of functionality.

00:15:18.390 --> 00:15:19.910
The first one is super simple.

00:15:19.910 --> 00:15:23.810
We're going to build drive
notifications for ourself.

00:15:23.810 --> 00:15:25.617
And so we've got a folder.

00:15:25.617 --> 00:15:27.950
We've asked everybody who's
presenting at the conference

00:15:27.950 --> 00:15:31.190
to put their decks in that
folder for review and approval.

00:15:31.190 --> 00:15:33.470
And we want to get notified
every time something

00:15:33.470 --> 00:15:35.000
new comes into that folder.

00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:39.151
Super simple use case, but
a good jumping off point.

00:15:39.151 --> 00:15:39.650
All right.

00:15:39.650 --> 00:15:41.540
So first thing that
we're going to do

00:15:41.540 --> 00:15:44.900
is we're going to grab the
ID of this folder in Drive.

00:15:44.900 --> 00:15:47.970
This is our special folder.

00:15:47.970 --> 00:15:52.220
And then we'll create
an Apps Script project.

00:15:52.220 --> 00:15:54.200
Sorry, I should go back.

00:15:54.200 --> 00:15:56.570
You can go directly to a
script.google.com or to create

00:15:56.570 --> 00:15:58.820
an Apps Script project in a
particular place in Drive,

00:15:58.820 --> 00:16:00.695
it's really handy to
just go to the new menu,

00:16:00.695 --> 00:16:02.367
click More, and then
Google Apps Script.

00:16:05.780 --> 00:16:06.290
All right.

00:16:06.290 --> 00:16:09.970
So obviously, you
memorized the folder ID,

00:16:09.970 --> 00:16:12.180
and then you recognize it here.

00:16:12.180 --> 00:16:15.890
So we've stashed
that in a global.

00:16:15.890 --> 00:16:19.350
And we're going to do some
processing on that folder.

00:16:19.350 --> 00:16:23.152
You'll see what we're going to
do on one run of this script

00:16:23.152 --> 00:16:24.860
as I step through the
code, and then I'll

00:16:24.860 --> 00:16:26.990
show you how we set it up
to run on a time trigger

00:16:26.990 --> 00:16:29.602
so you'll get those
notifications regularly.

00:16:29.602 --> 00:16:31.310
So the first thing
that we're going to do

00:16:31.310 --> 00:16:35.290
is we're going to grab a folder
object using the Drive App

00:16:35.290 --> 00:16:39.440
built-in API that represents
that folder that I just

00:16:39.440 --> 00:16:40.550
showed you in Drive.

00:16:40.550 --> 00:16:42.840
This lets you script that
folder in a lot of ways.

00:16:42.840 --> 00:16:44.381
And the way that
we're going to do it

00:16:44.381 --> 00:16:46.170
is to look at what's
inside that folder.

00:16:46.170 --> 00:16:49.100
And you'll see when we go
to install our trigger that

00:16:49.100 --> 00:16:51.890
runs this regularly,
you as the developer

00:16:51.890 --> 00:16:54.770
are going to get asked to
authorize access to Drive.

00:16:54.770 --> 00:16:57.200
And so everything that Apps
Script does, whether it's

00:16:57.200 --> 00:17:00.230
running as you the developer,
or as we'll see later

00:17:00.230 --> 00:17:02.570
on, if it's running as
the end user, everything

00:17:02.570 --> 00:17:04.650
is authorized by OAuth.

00:17:04.650 --> 00:17:07.400
And so you or the end user
know what data Apps Script

00:17:07.400 --> 00:17:10.090
is working with.

00:17:10.090 --> 00:17:10.589
All right.

00:17:10.589 --> 00:17:13.079
So get an iterator
over the files.

00:17:13.079 --> 00:17:16.619
We're also going to grab
our ScriptProperties.

00:17:16.619 --> 00:17:21.930
ScriptProperties is a store key
value pairs for this script.

00:17:21.930 --> 00:17:23.800
You also have user properties.

00:17:23.800 --> 00:17:26.130
And in the context of a
container bound script--

00:17:26.130 --> 00:17:27.690
a script attached
to a document--

00:17:27.690 --> 00:17:29.520
Document Properties.

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:34.140
And so this is your very
lightweight persistence layer

00:17:34.140 --> 00:17:34.997
for Apps Script.

00:17:34.997 --> 00:17:37.080
When we get to the roadmap,
I'll talk a little bit

00:17:37.080 --> 00:17:39.574
about our vision for
storage in Apps Script.

00:17:39.574 --> 00:17:40.990
But this is where
you start today.

00:17:40.990 --> 00:17:44.600
And it works great
for this use case.

00:17:44.600 --> 00:17:45.620
All right.

00:17:45.620 --> 00:17:47.710
So this is the while loop.

00:17:47.710 --> 00:17:50.540
I don't want a
single anybody out.

00:17:50.540 --> 00:17:52.235
Does everybody in
here know JavaScript

00:17:52.235 --> 00:17:53.360
like the back of your hand?

00:17:56.950 --> 00:17:57.600
OK.

00:17:57.600 --> 00:18:00.300
So right.

00:18:00.300 --> 00:18:01.650
This is a while loop.

00:18:01.650 --> 00:18:05.130
That iterates over a set of
code as long as the condition

00:18:05.130 --> 00:18:06.660
in the parentheses is true.

00:18:06.660 --> 00:18:09.721
And so file iterator
has this method

00:18:09.721 --> 00:18:12.220
that tells us whether it has
more files that we can look at.

00:18:12.220 --> 00:18:14.553
And we're just going to look
at each file in this folder

00:18:14.553 --> 00:18:15.780
until we run out.

00:18:19.650 --> 00:18:23.100
Our strategy here is
we want to know what

00:18:23.100 --> 00:18:24.900
new files are in this folder.

00:18:24.900 --> 00:18:26.982
That inherently is
a persistent problem

00:18:26.982 --> 00:18:29.190
because we have to know what
files we've seen before.

00:18:29.190 --> 00:18:31.440
And that's we're going to
use our property store for.

00:18:31.440 --> 00:18:35.760
And so we're forming a key with
the file ID, the file slash

00:18:35.760 --> 00:18:37.140
and the key name.

00:18:37.140 --> 00:18:38.502
That is-- that's arbitrary.

00:18:38.502 --> 00:18:39.960
That's up to you
as a developer how

00:18:39.960 --> 00:18:41.197
you want to define your key.

00:18:41.197 --> 00:18:42.780
This is with the
anticipation that you

00:18:42.780 --> 00:18:45.300
might put some other stuff
in that property store.

00:18:45.300 --> 00:18:47.640
And so you're just
name spacing it.

00:18:47.640 --> 00:18:50.550
And then you're going
to see if you've already

00:18:50.550 --> 00:18:51.530
put that key in before.

00:18:51.530 --> 00:18:53.760
If you have, that's because
the script ran before,

00:18:53.760 --> 00:18:56.940
and you already saw that
file, no action required.

00:18:56.940 --> 00:19:01.944
But if not, then you're
going to set the property--

00:19:01.944 --> 00:19:03.360
so you know you've
seen the file--

00:19:03.360 --> 00:19:05.568
and then you're going to
just push it on to an array.

00:19:05.568 --> 00:19:07.630
And so that push method
is just adding your file

00:19:07.630 --> 00:19:08.630
to your new files array.

00:19:12.240 --> 00:19:15.900
And then, lastly, if
you found any new files,

00:19:15.900 --> 00:19:17.136
we're going to log that.

00:19:17.136 --> 00:19:18.510
And I'll show you
what that looks

00:19:18.510 --> 00:19:20.610
like in the logger feature.

00:19:20.610 --> 00:19:21.990
And we'll sent off an email.

00:19:24.800 --> 00:19:28.386
So this is
demonstrating the script

00:19:28.386 --> 00:19:31.010
running as a standalone on Apps
Script I'll show you how to set

00:19:31.010 --> 00:19:32.880
up a trigger in a minute.

00:19:32.880 --> 00:19:35.270
But for now we're just going
to select the process method

00:19:35.270 --> 00:19:36.710
that we just defined
and hit play.

00:19:36.710 --> 00:19:39.770
It's going to run.

00:19:39.770 --> 00:19:42.350
Now go to the logs.

00:19:42.350 --> 00:19:46.490
And we see indeed new slides
were found in that folder.

00:19:46.490 --> 00:19:49.960
We've got two decks
in that folder.

00:19:49.960 --> 00:19:51.610
We go over to our Gmail.

00:19:51.610 --> 00:19:55.060
And we also see those two
presentations listed there.

00:19:57.771 --> 00:19:58.270
OK.

00:19:58.270 --> 00:20:02.571
So the last step to have
true folder notifications

00:20:02.571 --> 00:20:05.070
is that you don't want to have
to go and hit the Play button

00:20:05.070 --> 00:20:06.510
every five minutes.

00:20:06.510 --> 00:20:08.940
And so in the
resources menu, you'll

00:20:08.940 --> 00:20:12.480
find triggers for the project.

00:20:12.480 --> 00:20:14.782
Configure a trigger here.

00:20:14.782 --> 00:20:15.990
This is the permissions step.

00:20:15.990 --> 00:20:18.630
And so because you're the
person installing the trigger,

00:20:18.630 --> 00:20:20.220
the script is going
to run as you.

00:20:20.220 --> 00:20:24.460
You the person with access to
the folder that it operates on.

00:20:24.460 --> 00:20:26.250
So that's by design.

00:20:26.250 --> 00:20:27.780
And you'll need
authorized access.

00:20:27.780 --> 00:20:30.150
And so this is the part
where what kind of data

00:20:30.150 --> 00:20:32.850
this script is accessing.

00:20:32.850 --> 00:20:36.510
So it knows who I am, but most
importantly are these first two

00:20:36.510 --> 00:20:39.720
where we see that it's
going to be a look at Drive,

00:20:39.720 --> 00:20:43.230
and it's going to and it's
going to manage my email.

00:20:43.230 --> 00:20:45.690
In the roadmap
portion of the talk,

00:20:45.690 --> 00:20:47.460
I will talk about
what we're doing

00:20:47.460 --> 00:20:51.240
to let you tailor the
scopes more narrowly.

00:20:51.240 --> 00:20:54.600
And I want to call out that
this first scope is broad.

00:20:54.600 --> 00:20:57.930
This is full Gmail scope
where this script all it does

00:20:57.930 --> 00:20:58.986
is send an email.

00:20:58.986 --> 00:21:00.360
And so I'll talk
about what we're

00:21:00.360 --> 00:21:01.980
going to do to
make sure that you

00:21:01.980 --> 00:21:04.050
don't need to request
this scope when you just

00:21:04.050 --> 00:21:04.850
want to send email.

00:21:10.056 --> 00:21:11.430
Save that trigger
and off you go.

00:21:11.430 --> 00:21:14.150
So you now have folder
notifications at Drive.

00:21:14.150 --> 00:21:16.660
It's a small use case, but it's
also a small amount of code.

00:21:16.660 --> 00:21:17.660
And that's what we like.

00:21:17.660 --> 00:21:21.023
We want those easy
things to be easy.

00:21:21.023 --> 00:21:22.867
AUDIENCE: What was
the trigger again?

00:21:22.867 --> 00:21:24.720
Is it every time I
view that folder?

00:21:24.720 --> 00:21:26.428
PAUL MCREYNOLDS: This
was a time trigger.

00:21:26.428 --> 00:21:33.900
And so, in this case, we
selected the time driven type,

00:21:33.900 --> 00:21:36.480
and ran it every five minutes.

00:21:36.480 --> 00:21:38.210
There are a few other
kinds of triggers,

00:21:38.210 --> 00:21:40.560
some of which we'll talk
about, some of which we won't.

00:21:40.560 --> 00:21:42.570
But it's a very similar
mechanism to the one

00:21:42.570 --> 00:21:45.236
that you would use, for example,
to write a script that looks up

00:21:45.236 --> 00:21:47.280
a document, whether one
is given to you by user,

00:21:47.280 --> 00:21:49.310
or selected using the Drive--

00:21:49.310 --> 00:21:51.840
the Drive picker, et
cetera, and install

00:21:51.840 --> 00:21:54.630
a trigger on that document
to get notifications

00:21:54.630 --> 00:21:55.707
when it changes.

00:21:55.707 --> 00:21:58.040
But in this case, we're just
talking about a simple time

00:21:58.040 --> 00:22:00.900
driven trigger.

00:22:00.900 --> 00:22:04.830
I should say I feel free to
jump in and ask questions

00:22:04.830 --> 00:22:07.770
as you did, anytime.

00:22:07.770 --> 00:22:08.496
Yeah.

00:22:08.496 --> 00:22:12.464
AUDIENCE: Let's say you
have a domain, 15,000 user.

00:22:12.464 --> 00:22:14.448
I don't know, 100.

00:22:14.448 --> 00:22:19.904
I know there's quotas
for the use of apps.

00:22:19.904 --> 00:22:23.376
I found really--
well, I don't know,

00:22:23.376 --> 00:22:28.336
I'm not a developer-- but
difficult to calculate

00:22:28.336 --> 00:22:29.824
the triggers.

00:22:29.824 --> 00:22:34.288
If there's a lot of action
going on on that folder,

00:22:34.288 --> 00:22:41.728
how do I calculate the amount
of in and out transactions

00:22:41.728 --> 00:22:43.347
or whatever?

00:22:43.347 --> 00:22:44.930
PAUL MCREYNOLDS:
That's a great point.

00:22:44.930 --> 00:22:48.820
So I-- Linked in
our documentation,

00:22:48.820 --> 00:22:54.070
are the quotas for
each of our APIs.

00:22:54.070 --> 00:22:56.244
When you write a
script in this way that

00:22:56.244 --> 00:22:57.910
runs as the developer,
that is something

00:22:57.910 --> 00:23:00.580
you have to think about because
it will be the developers

00:23:00.580 --> 00:23:03.220
quota that will determine
how many of those calls

00:23:03.220 --> 00:23:04.810
you can make.

00:23:04.810 --> 00:23:06.680
That becomes an
architecture question.

00:23:06.680 --> 00:23:08.859
Now, it's very
unlikely, for example,

00:23:08.859 --> 00:23:10.900
that you as a developer
would write a script that

00:23:10.900 --> 00:23:13.540
was processing a folder that
was being accessed by everybody

00:23:13.540 --> 00:23:14.562
in the organization.

00:23:14.562 --> 00:23:17.020
On the other hand, you might
be writing an application that

00:23:17.020 --> 00:23:19.510
lets somebody configure
a folder or create

00:23:19.510 --> 00:23:22.359
a folder for which they wanted
to receive notifications.

00:23:22.359 --> 00:23:24.400
At that point, you'd be
writing a script probably

00:23:24.400 --> 00:23:26.460
that ran as that user.

00:23:26.460 --> 00:23:29.380
And it would be that user's
quota who is getting absorbed.

00:23:29.380 --> 00:23:32.680
These quotas are designed to be
permissive enough that you can

00:23:32.680 --> 00:23:35.060
get done what you need to do.

00:23:35.060 --> 00:23:37.270
And typically, your
architectural decision

00:23:37.270 --> 00:23:39.580
about who the script
should run as will dictate

00:23:39.580 --> 00:23:41.620
that it run as the
user in those cases

00:23:41.620 --> 00:23:44.140
where there are going to be a
lot of people impacted by it.

00:23:44.140 --> 00:23:46.360
But if you did find yourself
in a situation-- say,

00:23:46.360 --> 00:23:49.180
you were building a web
app that used your OAuth

00:23:49.180 --> 00:23:52.420
token to access some
company wide data

00:23:52.420 --> 00:23:54.130
and present it to the user.

00:23:54.130 --> 00:23:56.230
If that became a mission
critical application,

00:23:56.230 --> 00:23:57.370
you might have to
think about how

00:23:57.370 --> 00:23:58.994
you would have that
run as the end user

00:23:58.994 --> 00:24:02.710
instead, and use their quota.

00:24:02.710 --> 00:24:04.169
When we get to the
roadmap portion,

00:24:04.169 --> 00:24:05.709
I'll also talk about
some of the work

00:24:05.709 --> 00:24:07.975
that we're doing on quotas
to make them more flexible.

00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:12.500
OK.

00:24:12.500 --> 00:24:16.280
So in the second
step, we're going

00:24:16.280 --> 00:24:17.600
to contain our bound scripts.

00:24:17.600 --> 00:24:20.452
That's a script that's attached
to a particular document.

00:24:20.452 --> 00:24:22.160
We are still working
on the same context.

00:24:22.160 --> 00:24:23.326
We're planning a conference.

00:24:23.326 --> 00:24:25.220
We're still operating
on a folder in Drive,

00:24:25.220 --> 00:24:26.678
but we've also got
a sheet that has

00:24:26.678 --> 00:24:30.110
some metadata about the
decks, about the sessions that

00:24:30.110 --> 00:24:32.270
are reflected in that folder.

00:24:32.270 --> 00:24:34.490
And so for every
session we're going

00:24:34.490 --> 00:24:36.770
to have the title of the
session, which will also

00:24:36.770 --> 00:24:40.670
be the title of the document,
a link to the slides,

00:24:40.670 --> 00:24:44.090
a user name of the owner,
and then comma separated

00:24:44.090 --> 00:24:46.970
list of approvers and
people who have approved.

00:24:46.970 --> 00:24:49.970
And so if this is
used correctly,

00:24:49.970 --> 00:24:53.150
which I have coded no checks to
ensure that it is, but wanted

00:24:53.150 --> 00:24:56.180
to keep it simple,
the approvals column

00:24:56.180 --> 00:24:59.750
will be a subset of
the approvers column.

00:24:59.750 --> 00:25:03.500
That it is to say if John, and
Tim, and Lisa need to approve,

00:25:03.500 --> 00:25:05.390
and John and Lisa have
approved, then you'll

00:25:05.390 --> 00:25:07.880
see John, comma Tim,
comma Elisa in approvers,

00:25:07.880 --> 00:25:12.020
and John comma Elisa
in in approvals.

00:25:12.020 --> 00:25:12.830
All right.

00:25:12.830 --> 00:25:14.672
So you've set up your sheet.

00:25:14.672 --> 00:25:16.130
And you want to
write a script that

00:25:16.130 --> 00:25:18.430
has a special
relationship with a sheet.

00:25:18.430 --> 00:25:21.260
To do that you go to the Tools
menu and click Script Editor.

00:25:24.460 --> 00:25:25.490
All right.

00:25:25.490 --> 00:25:27.160
So here's our script.

00:25:27.160 --> 00:25:30.100
Firstly, we've got the
familiar folder ID.

00:25:30.100 --> 00:25:30.730
Global.

00:25:30.730 --> 00:25:32.560
We're still working
with that folder.

00:25:32.560 --> 00:25:36.235
And then we've defined some
globals for column indexes.

00:25:38.800 --> 00:25:41.954
Please speak up if you've
asked Apps Script before

00:25:41.954 --> 00:25:43.870
and you have other ways
of doing these things.

00:25:43.870 --> 00:25:45.940
There are many ways
to skin these cats.

00:25:45.940 --> 00:25:50.300
And we'd love to hear from other
folks how they do these things.

00:25:50.300 --> 00:25:52.234
The other thing that
we're doing here--

00:25:52.234 --> 00:25:53.650
and this is one
of the cool things

00:25:53.650 --> 00:25:55.330
about working with JavaScript.

00:25:55.330 --> 00:26:00.550
Gmail app is just a global
in the JavaScript namespace.

00:26:00.550 --> 00:26:03.010
I hear we're going
to stub it out.

00:26:03.010 --> 00:26:05.260
We're going to replace it
with our own implementation.

00:26:05.260 --> 00:26:07.210
And that lets us
write a script that

00:26:07.210 --> 00:26:10.090
works with a sensitive
API that sends email

00:26:10.090 --> 00:26:13.630
to people with the confidence
that the first time we run it

00:26:13.630 --> 00:26:15.805
if there's something
wrong, if there's a typo,

00:26:15.805 --> 00:26:17.680
we're going to be the
ones who see it instead

00:26:17.680 --> 00:26:19.630
of the recipient of the email.

00:26:19.630 --> 00:26:22.840
And so this is going to
leverage logger.log which

00:26:22.840 --> 00:26:25.360
is just a simple logging tool.

00:26:25.360 --> 00:26:27.730
I'll talk a little bit later
about more sophisticated

00:26:27.730 --> 00:26:28.330
logging.

00:26:28.330 --> 00:26:31.810
And then there will be
a session at 4:00 today

00:26:31.810 --> 00:26:35.260
that goes all the way down the
rabbit hole on our Stackdriver

00:26:35.260 --> 00:26:39.590
logging integration, and
that will be over in Moscone.

00:26:39.590 --> 00:26:41.990
But here is simply
stubbing out send email

00:26:41.990 --> 00:26:44.120
so that our script doesn't
actually send e-mails

00:26:44.120 --> 00:26:47.390
until we're ready for it.

00:26:47.390 --> 00:26:48.620
All right.

00:26:48.620 --> 00:26:50.320
So first thing that
we're going to do,

00:26:50.320 --> 00:26:52.580
we're going to call a
spreadsheetapp.getactivesheet.

00:26:52.580 --> 00:26:53.582
I call this out earlier.

00:26:53.582 --> 00:26:55.790
This is the way that you
get that document with which

00:26:55.790 --> 00:26:59.870
you have that
special relationship.

00:26:59.870 --> 00:27:02.240
I've abstracted
out find new files.

00:27:02.240 --> 00:27:03.896
It's the same code
we saw previously.

00:27:03.896 --> 00:27:05.520
It's going to leverage
property service

00:27:05.520 --> 00:27:08.640
to keep track of where we
are, and to identify new files

00:27:08.640 --> 00:27:12.570
in the folder identified above.

00:27:12.570 --> 00:27:14.370
The new code here
is that we're going

00:27:14.370 --> 00:27:16.250
to iterate over those
new files and we're

00:27:16.250 --> 00:27:19.210
going to add a row to the sheet
for every file that we see.

00:27:19.210 --> 00:27:21.570
And so the design
here is that you might

00:27:21.570 --> 00:27:22.810
update the sheet directly.

00:27:22.810 --> 00:27:24.490
But if not, you'll
get a row added

00:27:24.490 --> 00:27:26.760
as people drop their
slides into the folder.

00:27:29.670 --> 00:27:33.230
And then we're going to iterate
over all rows of the sheet.

00:27:33.230 --> 00:27:34.590
And we're going to do--

00:27:34.590 --> 00:27:37.170
we're going to use an
email based workflow.

00:27:37.170 --> 00:27:40.110
And so process row
is the relevant call.

00:27:40.110 --> 00:27:43.650
And I'll show you what it does
for each row of the sheet.

00:27:43.650 --> 00:27:46.670
Just jumping back
briefly for some minutia,

00:27:46.670 --> 00:27:52.080
and you'll notice that
rows are zero index,

00:27:52.080 --> 00:27:54.350
but we have a header row so
we're starting with row 1.

00:27:56.950 --> 00:27:58.760
I should say there are other--

00:27:58.760 --> 00:28:01.535
so we're calling
getDataRange().g

00:28:01.535 --> 00:28:04.660
etDisplayValues() That's going
to return two dimensional array

00:28:04.660 --> 00:28:07.690
of the values of the
cells in a sheet.

00:28:07.690 --> 00:28:10.360
There are other methods that
allow you to grab a range.

00:28:10.360 --> 00:28:11.530
Those are not zero indexed.

00:28:11.530 --> 00:28:13.087
Like sheets, those
are one index.

00:28:13.087 --> 00:28:15.670
But when you're operating on a
JavaScript array, zero indexed.

00:28:18.340 --> 00:28:19.270
All right.

00:28:19.270 --> 00:28:22.750
So here we're doing
some quick housekeeping.

00:28:22.750 --> 00:28:24.907
We're making sure
this is in good order.

00:28:24.907 --> 00:28:27.490
And so we're going to check that
there's an owner for the row.

00:28:27.490 --> 00:28:31.780
And so when we discover a new
set of slides in this folder,

00:28:31.780 --> 00:28:34.030
we're adding an
entry in our sheet

00:28:34.030 --> 00:28:36.250
that has the title and
a link to the slides.

00:28:36.250 --> 00:28:38.300
That obviously doesn't
have any of the metadata.

00:28:38.300 --> 00:28:39.670
That's only stored in Sheets.

00:28:39.670 --> 00:28:42.490
And so we need to let
somebody know that those rows

00:28:42.490 --> 00:28:45.700
need to be populated.

00:28:45.700 --> 00:28:48.520
And so we will
look for an owner.

00:28:48.520 --> 00:28:50.950
If there's no owner, then
we'll contact the owner

00:28:50.950 --> 00:28:53.160
of the script, which is you.

00:28:53.160 --> 00:28:55.470
Once we know the
owner, then we're

00:28:55.470 --> 00:29:01.040
going to look and see if we
have approvers for the slides.

00:29:01.040 --> 00:29:04.090
Did I do that wrong?

00:29:04.090 --> 00:29:04.590
OK.

00:29:04.590 --> 00:29:07.020
So edit.

00:29:07.020 --> 00:29:11.190
Approvers column index on
line 73 should be on line 64.

00:29:11.190 --> 00:29:15.120
And approvals column index
should be on line 73.

00:29:15.120 --> 00:29:17.787
Apologies.

00:29:17.787 --> 00:29:19.620
And then we're going
to check our approvals.

00:29:19.620 --> 00:29:22.020
And so this is the
actual business logic.

00:29:22.020 --> 00:29:24.750
This is the workflow here.

00:29:24.750 --> 00:29:31.950
So quick use of
JavaScript split function

00:29:31.950 --> 00:29:35.970
to split up our approvers
and our approvals values.

00:29:35.970 --> 00:29:39.070
So we have arrays of people who
need to approve this and people

00:29:39.070 --> 00:29:40.530
who have approved this.

00:29:40.530 --> 00:29:42.827
And then we're going to
iterate over our approvers.

00:29:42.827 --> 00:29:44.910
And we're going to check
whether they've approved.

00:29:44.910 --> 00:29:47.340
Any approver who
hasn't approved is

00:29:47.340 --> 00:29:49.265
going to get a
notification reminding them

00:29:49.265 --> 00:29:50.996
to hey, go check
out these slides,

00:29:50.996 --> 00:29:52.620
we need your sign
off for this session.

00:29:55.860 --> 00:29:56.360
OK.

00:29:56.360 --> 00:30:00.459
So now we've got some
data in the sheet.

00:30:00.459 --> 00:30:02.750
It may have been populated
automatically by our script.

00:30:02.750 --> 00:30:05.720
Or it may have been
populated manually.

00:30:05.720 --> 00:30:10.490
And we're going to add a
new session to our folder.

00:30:12.807 --> 00:30:15.140
Again, just showing you how
you would run this manually.

00:30:15.140 --> 00:30:17.860
We'll run the process function.

00:30:17.860 --> 00:30:20.860
Here, we get much more
substantial logging.

00:30:20.860 --> 00:30:25.630
These are log messages generated
by our send email stub.

00:30:25.630 --> 00:30:26.800
By our send stub.

00:30:26.800 --> 00:30:31.090
And so what you can see here is
that this is a lot of activity

00:30:31.090 --> 00:30:33.760
that would have happened in the
wild that would have generated

00:30:33.760 --> 00:30:36.940
real email messages if we
hadn't stubbed out this method.

00:30:36.940 --> 00:30:39.280
It's a great idea for
testing to put it in the logs

00:30:39.280 --> 00:30:41.110
first and make sure
that these messages say

00:30:41.110 --> 00:30:42.026
what you want them to.

00:30:44.340 --> 00:30:47.130
And we see that our
sheet has been updated.

00:30:47.130 --> 00:30:49.601
We've added a new row
with a link to slides.

00:30:49.601 --> 00:30:51.600
Also in those logs, you
would have seen messages

00:30:51.600 --> 00:30:54.360
going out to you
requesting you to populate

00:30:54.360 --> 00:30:55.920
the owner for this new row.

00:30:59.370 --> 00:31:00.480
All right.

00:31:00.480 --> 00:31:03.150
So in the last step
here, we're going

00:31:03.150 --> 00:31:07.750
to build a front end
for our workflow engine.

00:31:07.750 --> 00:31:11.310
And so we're going
to build some UI that

00:31:11.310 --> 00:31:13.230
is no longer running
as the developer,

00:31:13.230 --> 00:31:14.520
but that runs as the end user.

00:31:14.520 --> 00:31:17.400
Who's going to be somebody
who's involved in the conference

00:31:17.400 --> 00:31:23.310
that we're planning, and who has
approval responsibility for one

00:31:23.310 --> 00:31:25.251
or more of the presentations.

00:31:25.251 --> 00:31:27.000
We'll show them what
they need to approve,

00:31:27.000 --> 00:31:28.740
and we'll let them
approve in the UI

00:31:28.740 --> 00:31:30.626
instead of going
directly to the sheet.

00:31:33.602 --> 00:31:34.600
OK.

00:31:34.600 --> 00:31:36.750
So first thing that
we're going to do,

00:31:36.750 --> 00:31:38.440
we're going to add
a new script file

00:31:38.440 --> 00:31:42.550
to isolate the logic
related to our web app.

00:31:42.550 --> 00:31:44.100
This is going to
be a script file--

00:31:44.100 --> 00:31:45.490
we'll just call it web app--

00:31:45.490 --> 00:31:48.136
that defines a do get method.

00:31:48.136 --> 00:31:50.260
And sorry, it's covered up
by the orange box there.

00:31:50.260 --> 00:31:52.240
But that's the magic
name of a method

00:31:52.240 --> 00:31:55.770
and a web app that responds
to an HTTP get request.

00:31:55.770 --> 00:31:59.766
You can also define
a do post for posts.

00:31:59.766 --> 00:32:01.390
Again, we're grabbing
the active sheet.

00:32:05.380 --> 00:32:07.910
This do get method
does two things.

00:32:07.910 --> 00:32:11.920
It renders the list of sessions
that a person needs to approve.

00:32:11.920 --> 00:32:13.990
It also processes
the request that's

00:32:13.990 --> 00:32:17.830
generated when somebody
clicks the approved button.

00:32:17.830 --> 00:32:19.870
And so here, we'll
see some logic

00:32:19.870 --> 00:32:24.490
that's checking if an
approval is being granted.

00:32:24.490 --> 00:32:27.340
And that will be in the
form of a URL parameter

00:32:27.340 --> 00:32:29.920
with the document URL.

00:32:29.920 --> 00:32:31.920
And so we're going to
split up the document URL,

00:32:31.920 --> 00:32:34.930
and we're going to extract
the ID of the document that's

00:32:34.930 --> 00:32:35.620
being approved.

00:32:35.620 --> 00:32:39.040
And so the request coming
in to this would be

00:32:39.040 --> 00:32:42.430
your script's base
URL, question mark,

00:32:42.430 --> 00:32:46.960
and then your doc
parameter-- that's just doc

00:32:46.960 --> 00:32:48.820
as we've defined it--

00:32:48.820 --> 00:32:51.710
which will have a URL in it.

00:32:51.710 --> 00:32:53.950
Which will be the
URL of the slide deck

00:32:53.950 --> 00:32:55.810
that you are approving.

00:32:55.810 --> 00:32:58.300
We know the URL of
a slide deck has

00:32:58.300 --> 00:33:02.590
an ID for the document in it
before the trailing slash.

00:33:02.590 --> 00:33:04.960
So we'll split it up and
we'll take the second

00:33:04.960 --> 00:33:07.930
to last portion of that URL.

00:33:07.930 --> 00:33:10.070
And we know that
that's our document ID.

00:33:10.070 --> 00:33:11.950
So this is just how
we grab the document

00:33:11.950 --> 00:33:13.250
ID that's being approved.

00:33:16.310 --> 00:33:19.550
Then we're going to call
session dot get active user.

00:33:19.550 --> 00:33:20.970
And this is interesting.

00:33:20.970 --> 00:33:23.510
This is the part
where we identify

00:33:23.510 --> 00:33:26.120
the person at the keyboard.

00:33:26.120 --> 00:33:28.160
This is not you, the developer.

00:33:28.160 --> 00:33:30.620
But this is the person
accessing your web app.

00:33:30.620 --> 00:33:34.520
Because that person has a Google
login in and is signed in,

00:33:34.520 --> 00:33:36.497
their identity is already known.

00:33:36.497 --> 00:33:38.330
They will see an OAuth
prompt, because we're

00:33:38.330 --> 00:33:41.250
going to be looking at
some data on their behalf.

00:33:41.250 --> 00:33:45.682
But the log in flow is already
part of their Google login.

00:33:45.682 --> 00:33:47.390
And so we're going to
grab that identity,

00:33:47.390 --> 00:33:48.740
and we're going to
get their user name

00:33:48.740 --> 00:33:50.031
as part of their email address.

00:33:53.390 --> 00:33:54.320
All right.

00:33:54.320 --> 00:33:57.580
So now we're generating
our response.

00:33:57.580 --> 00:34:01.070
And the loop that we're about
to walk through does two things.

00:34:01.070 --> 00:34:03.440
If this is the document
that's being approved,

00:34:03.440 --> 00:34:05.360
we're going to
update the approvers.

00:34:05.360 --> 00:34:08.420
We're also going to add
every document to an array

00:34:08.420 --> 00:34:10.040
of approvable documents.

00:34:10.040 --> 00:34:11.790
And we're going to use
that to then render

00:34:11.790 --> 00:34:14.750
an HTML response that
shows the list of documents

00:34:14.750 --> 00:34:16.144
that this user needs to approve.

00:34:21.670 --> 00:34:22.449
All right.

00:34:22.449 --> 00:34:26.590
So this is an
example of using the

00:34:26.590 --> 00:34:28.690
get range method on a sheet.

00:34:28.690 --> 00:34:31.500
Those methods are one index.

00:34:31.500 --> 00:34:32.620
Not zero index.

00:34:32.620 --> 00:34:34.810
Or just an important
detail to remember.

00:34:34.810 --> 00:34:37.690
And we're going to grab
the approvers value

00:34:37.690 --> 00:34:39.510
for the current
row of the sheet.

00:34:39.510 --> 00:34:41.260
That's who needs to
approve this session.

00:34:44.870 --> 00:34:47.710
We've abstracted out
parsing the approvers.

00:34:47.710 --> 00:34:49.260
A little bit of a mouthful.

00:34:49.260 --> 00:34:52.010
But we're going to
split on commas.

00:34:52.010 --> 00:34:53.989
And for the empty
string, we just

00:34:53.989 --> 00:34:56.750
have to handle the case where
we wind up with an array,

00:34:56.750 --> 00:34:58.857
with one empty string in it.

00:34:58.857 --> 00:35:01.640
In that case, there
are no approvers.

00:35:01.640 --> 00:35:03.500
And so just splitting
up on commas.

00:35:10.350 --> 00:35:12.390
Here we're asking,
is the current user

00:35:12.390 --> 00:35:13.840
an approver of this document.

00:35:13.840 --> 00:35:16.310
So remember, we're iterating
over all rows of the sheet.

00:35:16.310 --> 00:35:18.570
We're only going to do
anything for those rows

00:35:18.570 --> 00:35:22.877
where this user needs
to approve our slides.

00:35:22.877 --> 00:35:24.460
And we're going to
grab the approvals.

00:35:27.000 --> 00:35:28.515
And then we're going to--

00:35:40.310 --> 00:35:41.900
why are we checking again here?

00:35:45.880 --> 00:35:47.201
Right.

00:35:47.201 --> 00:35:47.700
OK.

00:35:47.700 --> 00:35:51.660
So we are iterating
over our approvals.

00:35:51.660 --> 00:35:53.340
And we're seeing
if this approver

00:35:53.340 --> 00:35:56.550
has approved this document yet.

00:35:56.550 --> 00:35:59.820
If not, then we're
going to include it

00:35:59.820 --> 00:36:02.840
in the approvals list.

00:36:02.840 --> 00:36:03.340
Excuse me.

00:36:03.340 --> 00:36:06.000
We're going to add it to the
approvals list regardless.

00:36:06.000 --> 00:36:08.160
But we're also going to
stash a value that tells us

00:36:08.160 --> 00:36:09.284
whether it's been approved.

00:36:13.080 --> 00:36:14.400
All right.

00:36:14.400 --> 00:36:17.490
Lastly, we're going
to build an object.

00:36:17.490 --> 00:36:20.220
And so here, just for
every row in the sheet that

00:36:20.220 --> 00:36:22.650
represents a document that
needs somebody's approval,

00:36:22.650 --> 00:36:25.200
we're going to have an entry
and an array that tells us

00:36:25.200 --> 00:36:27.630
the title, URL, whether
it's been approved,

00:36:27.630 --> 00:36:30.870
and then the URL that you would
have to get to approve it.

00:36:30.870 --> 00:36:33.189
And that's the URL
with the doc parameter

00:36:33.189 --> 00:36:34.980
that we know how to
process, so that we can

00:36:34.980 --> 00:36:36.229
approve an entry in the sheet.

00:36:39.920 --> 00:36:40.420
OK.

00:36:40.420 --> 00:36:41.810
Cool.

00:36:41.810 --> 00:36:44.900
We'll look next at
how we do templating.

00:36:44.900 --> 00:36:48.800
So here, we are grabbing
our HTML template.

00:36:48.800 --> 00:36:52.520
And we're shoving the data in
approvables into that template,

00:36:52.520 --> 00:36:53.810
and rendering it.

00:36:53.810 --> 00:36:56.180
This is just a
classic separation

00:36:56.180 --> 00:36:59.690
of controller and view, of
business logic from the view.

00:36:59.690 --> 00:37:02.161
And so here we know what
our approvables are.

00:37:02.161 --> 00:37:04.160
And we know we're going
to write a template that

00:37:04.160 --> 00:37:06.990
knows how to render
those out to the client.

00:37:06.990 --> 00:37:08.870
And so we write this out.

00:37:08.870 --> 00:37:10.010
It's boilerplate.

00:37:10.010 --> 00:37:11.540
We call it template.evaluate.

00:37:11.540 --> 00:37:13.790
And that's going to
return our template HTML

00:37:13.790 --> 00:37:14.725
with values populated.

00:37:14.725 --> 00:37:16.850
So now I'll show you what
that template looks like.

00:37:20.430 --> 00:37:20.930
All right.

00:37:20.930 --> 00:37:24.024
So you'll create
a new HTML file.

00:37:24.024 --> 00:37:24.815
We'll call it page.

00:37:29.470 --> 00:37:31.250
This part gets
written out for you.

00:37:31.250 --> 00:37:33.040
Basic HTML.

00:37:33.040 --> 00:37:36.010
The interesting part here
is the code that we write.

00:37:36.010 --> 00:37:38.192
And so we're going to
render a table that

00:37:38.192 --> 00:37:40.400
has a list of the documents
that need to be approved.

00:37:40.400 --> 00:37:42.066
It's going to have
an action button that

00:37:42.066 --> 00:37:44.310
lets you as the approver
approve a document.

00:37:46.990 --> 00:37:51.430
So here what you're saying
is JavaScript code embedded

00:37:51.430 --> 00:37:54.250
in HTML, angle
bracket, question mark

00:37:54.250 --> 00:37:56.950
is the escape
sequence to do that.

00:37:56.950 --> 00:38:04.890
And so approvables
here, if you'll recall,

00:38:04.890 --> 00:38:07.080
we've passed in to our template.

00:38:07.080 --> 00:38:11.350
And so properties
of the template

00:38:11.350 --> 00:38:15.490
are accessible from within
your HTML template code

00:38:15.490 --> 00:38:18.640
as JavaScript variables
using that escape sequence.

00:38:18.640 --> 00:38:23.920
And so template.approvables
sets that approvables array

00:38:23.920 --> 00:38:25.940
on the template.

00:38:25.940 --> 00:38:28.670
And it's this for loop here
that iterates over those

00:38:28.670 --> 00:38:31.220
approvables.

00:38:31.220 --> 00:38:33.950
Now once we close the escape
sequence, we're back in HTML.

00:38:33.950 --> 00:38:36.350
And so we're just
writing out a tr

00:38:36.350 --> 00:38:38.010
for every document in our list.

00:38:41.040 --> 00:38:43.770
And then we're going
to render the Control

00:38:43.770 --> 00:38:45.390
button to approve the document.

00:38:45.390 --> 00:38:48.910
Or we'll render out a checkbox
if it's already been approved.

00:38:48.910 --> 00:38:55.170
And so you'll see here in the
second block of the statement,

00:38:55.170 --> 00:38:57.370
if this document
hasn't been approved,

00:38:57.370 --> 00:38:59.580
then we're going to
render an empty check box.

00:38:59.580 --> 00:39:02.910
And that's going to link to that
approval URL that identifies

00:39:02.910 --> 00:39:04.740
this document as the
one that you-- again,

00:39:04.740 --> 00:39:08.104
you, the end user of the
application-- want to approve.

00:39:08.104 --> 00:39:09.520
And then we're
going to render out

00:39:09.520 --> 00:39:10.940
the title with a
link to the document

00:39:10.940 --> 00:39:12.023
so that you can review it.

00:39:14.760 --> 00:39:16.840
Close our curly braces.

00:39:16.840 --> 00:39:17.340
OK.

00:39:17.340 --> 00:39:20.370
So in the Publish menu,
you'll find Deploy as Web App.

00:39:20.370 --> 00:39:23.280
This is how you tell Apps Script
that you want this code to run

00:39:23.280 --> 00:39:26.040
in response to HTTP requests.

00:39:26.040 --> 00:39:27.720
And to give you a
well-defined URL

00:39:27.720 --> 00:39:30.520
that users can go to
use your application.

00:39:30.520 --> 00:39:31.730
This is all it takes.

00:39:31.730 --> 00:39:35.281
You write some HTML, you
write some business logic,

00:39:35.281 --> 00:39:36.780
and then you deploy
it as a web app,

00:39:36.780 --> 00:39:41.190
and we'll tell you where users
go to access that application.

00:39:41.190 --> 00:39:44.130
So a couple of key things here.

00:39:44.130 --> 00:39:46.970
Execute the app as the
user accessing the web app.

00:39:46.970 --> 00:39:49.920
This is designed to be
a user centric view.

00:39:49.920 --> 00:39:51.900
And this gets to your
question as well.

00:39:51.900 --> 00:39:54.090
This is a case where
your quota is not going

00:39:54.090 --> 00:39:55.650
to be absorbed as a developer.

00:39:55.650 --> 00:39:58.720
If this were not conference
planning but vacation planning,

00:39:58.720 --> 00:40:01.320
and everybody in the
company were using it,

00:40:01.320 --> 00:40:03.840
then you would only be
absorbing end users' quota

00:40:03.840 --> 00:40:06.590
as they hit it.

00:40:06.590 --> 00:40:08.270
And then who has
access to the app?

00:40:08.270 --> 00:40:11.030
We're going to allow
everybody in the domain

00:40:11.030 --> 00:40:15.350
to access the application.

00:40:15.350 --> 00:40:16.700
And then we get our URL.

00:40:16.700 --> 00:40:19.970
And so this is the
application URL.

00:40:19.970 --> 00:40:23.900
You may choose to map this
with a URL shortener, or even

00:40:23.900 --> 00:40:25.460
a subdomain.

00:40:25.460 --> 00:40:29.964
That's one way that we
see folks kind of putting

00:40:29.964 --> 00:40:31.880
the stamp of approval
on an application that's

00:40:31.880 --> 00:40:34.130
built using Apps Script.

00:40:34.130 --> 00:40:37.160
So that folks know that it's
sanctioned by IT, by the folks

00:40:37.160 --> 00:40:38.840
who control the DNS record.

00:40:41.731 --> 00:40:42.230
All right.

00:40:42.230 --> 00:40:46.460
So now we're experiencing this
web application as a user.

00:40:46.460 --> 00:40:49.170
The first step is going to be to
review the permissions that it

00:40:49.170 --> 00:40:50.480
requires.

00:40:50.480 --> 00:40:53.240
Again, I'll talk about
how we're working on this.

00:40:53.240 --> 00:40:56.050
This is a long list
of permissions.

00:40:56.050 --> 00:40:57.920
The big ones,
though, that you see

00:40:57.920 --> 00:41:01.227
are Gmail, Drive, and View
and Mange Spreadsheets.

00:41:04.759 --> 00:41:05.800
And so here's what I see.

00:41:05.800 --> 00:41:08.650
These are the presentations
that need my approval, where

00:41:08.650 --> 00:41:10.660
I'm in that list of approvers.

00:41:10.660 --> 00:41:12.460
And neither of
these is approved.

00:41:12.460 --> 00:41:17.050
And so I have a checkbox
next to each of them.

00:41:17.050 --> 00:41:18.640
After reviewing
this presentation,

00:41:18.640 --> 00:41:20.560
I click the checkbox.

00:41:20.560 --> 00:41:22.360
Page loads.

00:41:22.360 --> 00:41:24.070
We render again.

00:41:24.070 --> 00:41:25.570
And this time we
render a checkmark

00:41:25.570 --> 00:41:28.028
instead of an open box because
I've approved this document.

00:41:31.920 --> 00:41:34.590
And here we see I've been
added to the approvals

00:41:34.590 --> 00:41:35.560
column of the document.

00:41:35.560 --> 00:41:37.860
So the spreadsheet
is up to date.

00:41:37.860 --> 00:41:40.800
What we've really done here is
we've replaced the spreadsheet

00:41:40.800 --> 00:41:43.620
as an interface to our
application with a web

00:41:43.620 --> 00:41:46.184
application that's
tailored to the use case.

00:41:46.184 --> 00:41:47.850
And it also prevents
the user from doing

00:41:47.850 --> 00:41:49.641
stuff they ought not
to do with this sheet.

00:41:53.750 --> 00:41:54.250
Great.

00:41:54.250 --> 00:42:02.770
So that was a walk-through
of three key deployment types

00:42:02.770 --> 00:42:05.200
in the context of one use case.

00:42:05.200 --> 00:42:08.800
And just wanted to quickly
touch on stuff that didn't get

00:42:08.800 --> 00:42:09.970
swept into it.

00:42:09.970 --> 00:42:11.290
Because there really is a lot.

00:42:11.290 --> 00:42:14.260
This product has been
around since 2009.

00:42:14.260 --> 00:42:16.690
And it's grown significantly.

00:42:16.690 --> 00:42:20.740
So built-in APIs, we
saw a few of these.

00:42:20.740 --> 00:42:23.380
We saw Drive app, we
saw Spreadsheet app.

00:42:23.380 --> 00:42:26.680
There about a dozen of
these built-in APIs.

00:42:26.680 --> 00:42:30.280
They are designed to be
ergonomic and accessible

00:42:30.280 --> 00:42:33.190
to people who don't
write a ton of code.

00:42:33.190 --> 00:42:37.600
In most cases, those APIs
mirror and are basically

00:42:37.600 --> 00:42:41.920
wrappers around a
public rest HTTP API

00:42:41.920 --> 00:42:44.620
that you can use to
talk to these services.

00:42:44.620 --> 00:42:48.340
We've packaged them up in
an ergonomic, easy way.

00:42:48.340 --> 00:42:51.020
If you're familiar
with J2EE Beans then

00:42:51.020 --> 00:42:56.380
the method naming pattern
will be super familiar.

00:42:56.380 --> 00:43:00.790
It's a great way to
quickly automate G Suite.

00:43:00.790 --> 00:43:03.730
We also give you access to
other Google and GCP APIs

00:43:03.730 --> 00:43:05.530
as advanced services.

00:43:05.530 --> 00:43:08.860
Those are APIs where you
do effectively hit them

00:43:08.860 --> 00:43:12.670
in the existing RESTful form.

00:43:12.670 --> 00:43:15.190
We just put some niceties
around them in Apps Script

00:43:15.190 --> 00:43:16.930
to make it easier
to make those calls.

00:43:19.630 --> 00:43:24.094
We define google.script.run.

00:43:24.094 --> 00:43:25.510
This is in the
context of add-ons.

00:43:25.510 --> 00:43:28.057
Specifically, again, if you're
around for the next session,

00:43:28.057 --> 00:43:29.890
you'll see how this can
get used to generate

00:43:29.890 --> 00:43:33.850
a call from the client side that
runs code on the server side.

00:43:33.850 --> 00:43:35.830
This is also what you
would use in App Maker

00:43:35.830 --> 00:43:38.260
if you wanted to run a server
side script in response

00:43:38.260 --> 00:43:39.370
to a client event.

00:43:39.370 --> 00:43:42.010
Super easy to call any
function that you've defined

00:43:42.010 --> 00:43:45.030
in Apps Script from the client.

00:43:45.030 --> 00:43:48.070
The 04:00 session over in
Moscone, we'll dive deep.

00:43:48.070 --> 00:43:50.770
Our Stackdriver
Logging integration

00:43:50.770 --> 00:43:52.840
is currently in EAP.

00:43:52.840 --> 00:43:54.700
Folks who are in
the App Maker EAP

00:43:54.700 --> 00:43:56.950
will have access
to this feature.

00:43:56.950 --> 00:44:01.870
And it lets you log messages
using the familiar console.log

00:44:01.870 --> 00:44:02.620
method.

00:44:02.620 --> 00:44:06.020
And those messages go
to Stack driver logging.

00:44:06.020 --> 00:44:10.300
And there, they're available
for analysis and for monitoring.

00:44:10.300 --> 00:44:13.639
It's a super powerful tool for
monitoring your application.

00:44:13.639 --> 00:44:15.430
And I would recommend
it highly for anybody

00:44:15.430 --> 00:44:16.930
who's doing something
sophisticated

00:44:16.930 --> 00:44:18.280
or that has lots of users.

00:44:18.280 --> 00:44:20.020
Logger.log becomes
cumbersome when

00:44:20.020 --> 00:44:23.100
you're trying to work with
more than one end user.

00:44:23.100 --> 00:44:24.980
Add-ons for docs,
sheets and forms.

00:44:24.980 --> 00:44:26.770
That's the next session.

00:44:26.770 --> 00:44:28.870
Custom menu items,
sidebars, and dialogs

00:44:28.870 --> 00:44:30.160
for doc sheets and forms.

00:44:30.160 --> 00:44:32.710
And so you can define
these in an add-on

00:44:32.710 --> 00:44:34.780
if you want everybody
to be able install them.

00:44:34.780 --> 00:44:36.490
Or if you just want
to build some UI

00:44:36.490 --> 00:44:39.180
around a particular
document, you can--

00:44:39.180 --> 00:44:40.930
you still have access
to the same methods.

00:44:40.930 --> 00:44:42.250
You can add menu items.

00:44:42.250 --> 00:44:45.490
You can add a side bar
that has your custom UI.

00:44:45.490 --> 00:44:48.310
You can trick your sheet
out to your heart's

00:44:48.310 --> 00:44:53.980
content with these custom
UI elements in Sheets.

00:44:53.980 --> 00:44:55.900
You can find custom
functions for sheets.

00:44:55.900 --> 00:45:00.410
We see that's used a lot in
vertical-specific use cases,

00:45:00.410 --> 00:45:03.280
especially around finance,
but others as well,

00:45:03.280 --> 00:45:06.490
to extend sheets just with
custom functions that do stuff

00:45:06.490 --> 00:45:08.859
that the built-ins don't.

00:45:08.859 --> 00:45:10.400
Both event and
time-based triggering.

00:45:10.400 --> 00:45:12.340
We talked a little bit
about both of those.

00:45:12.340 --> 00:45:15.070
And then you can use
our execution API

00:45:15.070 --> 00:45:17.230
with an OAuth token that
you obtain or generate

00:45:17.230 --> 00:45:20.430
for the user that runs in
the context of that OAuth

00:45:20.430 --> 00:45:23.440
token, the outstrip code
that you've defined.

00:45:23.440 --> 00:45:26.470
So this is a way that you would
build a RESTful web service

00:45:26.470 --> 00:45:30.400
with Apps Script
that does something

00:45:30.400 --> 00:45:33.400
more than existing G
Suite APIs do for you.

00:45:33.400 --> 00:45:36.160
That interleaves
functionality of G Suite APIs,

00:45:36.160 --> 00:45:39.125
or possibly brings data in from
an on-prem or a third party

00:45:39.125 --> 00:45:39.625
source.

00:45:44.212 --> 00:45:46.170
Here's the full list of,
on the left hand side,

00:45:46.170 --> 00:45:48.150
built-in services.

00:45:48.150 --> 00:45:52.380
Some additional script services
for programming primitives

00:45:52.380 --> 00:45:54.630
and JDBC.

00:45:54.630 --> 00:45:56.940
And advanced services.

00:45:56.940 --> 00:45:59.940
These are RESTful APIs that
are scriptable in Apps Script

00:45:59.940 --> 00:46:03.810
without using [INAUDIBLE] fetch
to do explicit HTTP requests.

00:46:07.800 --> 00:46:08.790
All right.

00:46:08.790 --> 00:46:11.900
The fun part.

00:46:11.900 --> 00:46:15.440
The lawyers literally
made me put this up here.

00:46:15.440 --> 00:46:17.632
This is the usual.

00:46:17.632 --> 00:46:19.090
This is where we
think we're going,

00:46:19.090 --> 00:46:21.006
and the timeline that
we think we're doing it.

00:46:21.006 --> 00:46:22.820
No formal commitment.

00:46:22.820 --> 00:46:24.530
But the stuff we're
doing is important.

00:46:24.530 --> 00:46:29.550
So I hope we do it on
the days you see here.

00:46:29.550 --> 00:46:32.360
So the first thing
that we're working on

00:46:32.360 --> 00:46:36.830
is a step toward improving
developer and end user

00:46:36.830 --> 00:46:40.250
visibility into the
behavior of Apps Script.

00:46:40.250 --> 00:46:43.790
Often, your scripts run in
the background in response

00:46:43.790 --> 00:46:46.550
to triggers that
don't have a UI.

00:46:46.550 --> 00:46:48.680
And whether to bug them
or just to make sure

00:46:48.680 --> 00:46:50.390
that everything's
going well, you

00:46:50.390 --> 00:46:52.820
want to see basically
your top, you

00:46:52.820 --> 00:46:54.650
want to see your process list.

00:46:54.650 --> 00:46:57.580
And so this is something
that's currently in TT.

00:46:57.580 --> 00:46:59.810
You can go and see all
of the scripts that

00:46:59.810 --> 00:47:01.400
are running as you--

00:47:01.400 --> 00:47:06.240
various filters available
with their result. This

00:47:06.240 --> 00:47:07.550
It's great for debugging.

00:47:07.550 --> 00:47:11.690
But it's also great for as
an end user, understanding

00:47:11.690 --> 00:47:13.760
what's going on
with the application

00:47:13.760 --> 00:47:17.490
that you use in your day to day.

00:47:17.490 --> 00:47:21.420
We're working on a new
management and deployment API.

00:47:21.420 --> 00:47:24.480
This is geared toward
more sophisticated users

00:47:24.480 --> 00:47:27.210
of Apps Script and more
sophisticated workflows.

00:47:27.210 --> 00:47:31.085
This is the way that you would
use your own developer tools

00:47:31.085 --> 00:47:32.460
and in particular
your own source

00:47:32.460 --> 00:47:34.770
code management
with Apps Script.

00:47:34.770 --> 00:47:37.740
This API will let you push
scripts in to Apps Script,

00:47:37.740 --> 00:47:41.010
and deploy your Apps Script
project programmatically.

00:47:41.010 --> 00:47:44.340
You can use this also to
do continuous integration

00:47:44.340 --> 00:47:47.840
and automated testing
with Apps Script.

00:47:47.840 --> 00:47:51.420
Write your code, commit
to version control,

00:47:51.420 --> 00:47:54.990
push it up to your dev server,
deploy your dev server,

00:47:54.990 --> 00:47:56.850
make sure it works,
push that code up

00:47:56.850 --> 00:47:59.250
to your production server,
and deploy production server

00:47:59.250 --> 00:48:00.220
with the new code.

00:48:03.000 --> 00:48:03.940
Explicit OAuth.

00:48:03.940 --> 00:48:06.450
So this is something I
mentioned a couple of times.

00:48:06.450 --> 00:48:08.700
Today, Apps Script
infers the OAuth scopes

00:48:08.700 --> 00:48:11.610
that are required to do the
things that the script does.

00:48:11.610 --> 00:48:13.350
And so if you use
Drive app, you're

00:48:13.350 --> 00:48:14.850
going to request Drive scope.

00:48:14.850 --> 00:48:18.150
If you use Gmail app, today,
we request full Gmail scope.

00:48:18.150 --> 00:48:21.450
This is a way that you can
be more narrowly tailored.

00:48:21.450 --> 00:48:24.600
And it's just a first
step toward being better

00:48:24.600 --> 00:48:27.480
about requesting kind
of a minimum of access

00:48:27.480 --> 00:48:28.680
that an application needs.

00:48:28.680 --> 00:48:30.930
Which is something that
everybody cares about.

00:48:30.930 --> 00:48:33.180
This first step, you'll be
able to list out the scopes

00:48:33.180 --> 00:48:36.120
that you want as
an override to our

00:48:36.120 --> 00:48:37.580
automatically determining them.

00:48:37.580 --> 00:48:40.080
So you'll be able to narrowly
tailor those scopes to exactly

00:48:40.080 --> 00:48:41.580
what your script is doing.

00:48:44.810 --> 00:48:49.970
Just announced this morning,
Gmail add-ons is in TT.

00:48:49.970 --> 00:48:52.400
Like docs, sheets,
and forms add-ons,

00:48:52.400 --> 00:48:55.400
Gmail add-ons will be
written in Apps Script.

00:48:55.400 --> 00:48:58.100
Unlike docs, sheets,
and forms, Gmail add-ons

00:48:58.100 --> 00:49:02.930
use a domain specific
markup to render cards.

00:49:02.930 --> 00:49:06.260
And the cool thing about that
is that Gmail has written

00:49:06.260 --> 00:49:07.670
the code that renders the card.

00:49:07.670 --> 00:49:10.400
And that code runs on
the web and in mobile.

00:49:10.400 --> 00:49:13.010
And so you as a developer
of a Gmail add-on

00:49:13.010 --> 00:49:15.130
don't have to think about
what's your UI going

00:49:15.130 --> 00:49:17.390
to look like in all
these different contexts.

00:49:17.390 --> 00:49:20.750
You just render a card
using their markup.

00:49:20.750 --> 00:49:24.610
And it displays images
or links or lists.

00:49:24.610 --> 00:49:27.080
Lets you add a bunch of
richness around a message

00:49:27.080 --> 00:49:30.290
that matches your criteria
for running the add-on.

00:49:30.290 --> 00:49:31.660
We're really excited about this.

00:49:34.240 --> 00:49:36.380
SlidesApp and Slides add-ons.

00:49:36.380 --> 00:49:39.880
So we're going to have a built
in API for scripting slides.

00:49:39.880 --> 00:49:40.630
It's long overdue.

00:49:40.630 --> 00:49:43.630
We're really excited
to have it landing.

00:49:43.630 --> 00:49:46.720
And also the ability
to build add-ons

00:49:46.720 --> 00:49:48.250
for Slides in the
same way that you

00:49:48.250 --> 00:49:50.590
do for docs, sheets, and forms.

00:49:50.590 --> 00:49:53.950
There is a short link here if
you want to sign up for the TT.

00:49:53.950 --> 00:49:59.190
If you use slides heavily,
please give it a look.

00:49:59.190 --> 00:50:00.420
Batch and parallel jobs.

00:50:00.420 --> 00:50:04.600
This is partly in response
to quota concerns.

00:50:04.600 --> 00:50:06.690
One of the things
that developers

00:50:06.690 --> 00:50:09.720
run into when they try to
do big jobs in Apps Script

00:50:09.720 --> 00:50:11.760
is the 30 minute or
six minute execution

00:50:11.760 --> 00:50:15.270
time limit, depending on what
context you're running in.

00:50:15.270 --> 00:50:19.092
We are building out a service
that lets us still maintain

00:50:19.092 --> 00:50:21.300
some of those important
assumptions about the longest

00:50:21.300 --> 00:50:23.370
that a script
could possibly run,

00:50:23.370 --> 00:50:25.616
but while letting you
kick off a big job

00:50:25.616 --> 00:50:27.240
and not have to worry
about how it gets

00:50:27.240 --> 00:50:29.190
split up between Apps
Script processes.

00:50:29.190 --> 00:50:31.140
And so you'll pass
in an array of tasks

00:50:31.140 --> 00:50:32.667
that are just units of work.

00:50:32.667 --> 00:50:34.500
You'll tell us what
function you want to run

00:50:34.500 --> 00:50:35.890
on each of those units of work.

00:50:35.890 --> 00:50:37.431
And then you'll tell
us what function

00:50:37.431 --> 00:50:40.560
you want to run with the results
of each of those units of work.

00:50:40.560 --> 00:50:42.900
And we'll split that up
among Apps Script tasks.

00:50:42.900 --> 00:50:45.510
We'll parallelize them to
the extent that we can.

00:50:45.510 --> 00:50:48.930
This is intended to serve the
use case of large batch jobs

00:50:48.930 --> 00:50:53.350
that would normally take a long
time or consume a lot of quota.

00:50:53.350 --> 00:50:56.350
And then lastly, and a
little bit further out,

00:50:56.350 --> 00:51:01.301
we're thinking about more
developer and admin visibility.

00:51:01.301 --> 00:51:03.550
And that's kind of a high
level view of script health,

00:51:03.550 --> 00:51:04.960
without having to
dive into logs,

00:51:04.960 --> 00:51:07.730
or just look at the
most recent execution.

00:51:07.730 --> 00:51:10.180
To point you into
the execution history

00:51:10.180 --> 00:51:11.689
you see in the
process dashboard,

00:51:11.689 --> 00:51:14.230
but to let you easily navigate
from lots of different scripts

00:51:14.230 --> 00:51:14.830
you work with.

00:51:14.830 --> 00:51:17.080
See adoption of
your scripts, get

00:51:17.080 --> 00:51:19.960
notified of
deprecations, et cetera.

00:51:19.960 --> 00:51:22.480
We are in the earlier
phases of that project.

00:51:22.480 --> 00:51:26.260
But we think it's super
important to let developers--

00:51:26.260 --> 00:51:29.530
and the downline admins
have a lot of visibility

00:51:29.530 --> 00:51:32.110
into what's happening in their
domain, whether their scripts

00:51:32.110 --> 00:51:34.760
are working, whether they're
calling deprecated APIs

00:51:34.760 --> 00:51:35.562
and so forth.

00:51:39.630 --> 00:51:41.140
All right.

00:51:41.140 --> 00:51:45.670
Just a few quick
links to resources.

00:51:45.670 --> 00:51:48.620
Depending what level
you're working at,

00:51:48.620 --> 00:51:55.420
a great intro here first on the
list, thanks to our own Wesley,

00:51:55.420 --> 00:51:57.250
is at 1sXeuD.

00:52:00.580 --> 00:52:03.280
I'll leave that up for a second
so folks can snap a photo

00:52:03.280 --> 00:52:06.760
